CLOSING = """<ul>
<li><strong>Best structurally supported today: BTTS.</strong> Well-suited on <strong>Monza&ndash;Sassuolo</strong> (eight league matches between the two clubs this season, eight both-teams-to-score, no clean sheet and no blank for either), on <strong>Brentford&ndash;Chelsea</strong> (Chelsea four from four this season on top of 63.2% across 38 last), and on <strong>Espanyol&ndash;Elche</strong> (Elche 83.3% this season and 71.1% last, with failed-to-score rates of 16.7% and 13.2%, the lowest on the board). OK on the other two. It is the only family that is Dangerous nowhere, because clean-sheet and failed-to-score series are published in full by all five divisions for both seasons.</li>
<li><strong>Next best: totals.</strong> Well-suited on <strong>Bayern&ndash;Union</strong>, where the Bundesliga&rsquo;s 3.85 goals and 81.5% over 2.5 sit on top of Bayern&rsquo;s 94.1% across 34 matches last season and three from three for Union, and on <strong>Brentford&ndash;Chelsea</strong>, where Chelsea have gone over in all four this season after 65.8% in 38. OK on the other three, in each case because the two clubs&rsquo; own rates disagree or one of them was set in a different division.</li>
<li><strong>Least supported: the Asian handicap and draw-no-bet, Dangerous on all five cards.</strong> Seven of the eight league matches played by Brentford and Chelsea finished within a goal or level; Monza and Sassuolo have the same pattern; Monaco sit second on 10 points with fewer shots and less xG than they concede while Lens sit tenth with the best chance-creation numbers on the board; Bayern&rsquo;s three league margins are +4, 0 and +1; and five of Elche&rsquo;s six matches were within a goal with the sixth a 0&ndash;5. <strong>The 1X2 is Dangerous on three</strong> &mdash; Monza&ndash;Sassuolo, Monaco&ndash;Lens and Brentford&ndash;Chelsea &mdash; and only OK on the two where one side&rsquo;s record is genuinely continuous.</li>
<li><strong>Widest spreads: discipline and corners.</strong> Discipline is <strong>Well-suited on Brentford&ndash;Chelsea</strong> (Brentford 15.5 fouls per match, Madley at 4.6 fouls per card this season, the Premier League the most heavily carded of the five divisions at 3.98) and <strong>on Espanyol&ndash;Elche</strong> (Espanyol 17.67 fouls, the highest on the board, LaLiga at 4.46 cards, the official steady at 4.45&ndash;4.70 across 71 matches), and <strong>Dangerous on Monza&ndash;Sassuolo</strong>, where Perenzoni has five Serie A matches in three seasons and a Serie B record from a division carrying 47% more cards. Corners are <strong>Well-suited on Bayern&ndash;Union and Monaco&ndash;Lens</strong>, where one club&rsquo;s surplus holds across both seasons with a visible mechanism, and <strong>Dangerous on Espanyol&ndash;Elche</strong>, where Espanyol&rsquo;s count has halved to 2.17 per match with their shots and possession unchanged.</li>
<li><strong>There is no normal fixture on this board.</strong> Every one of the five contains at least one club whose head coach was not in charge in May. The closest is <strong>Espanyol&ndash;Elche</strong>, where both clubs played in this division last season and both have six league matches already played &mdash; the deepest current sample here &mdash; but Elche changed coach in June, which is a correction to what this page first said. Everything else is a club that changed coach, changed division, or has three or four matches on the clock.</li>
<li><strong>Where the evidence is thinnest, and which part of the board to trust least.</strong> First, <strong>Bayern&ndash;Union</strong>: three league matches per club, the smallest sample on the slate, and each club&rsquo;s three contains one result that distorts every rate built on it &mdash; Bayern&rsquo;s 0&ndash;0 at Schalke and Union&rsquo;s 0&ndash;4 at Leverkusen, now compounded by both Friedrich and Nsoki being confirmed out. Second, <strong>the referees&rsquo; penalty rates, which are unpublished for all five officials</strong> in every source reached, so no Discipline row on this board can speak to contact in the box. Third, <strong>Monaco&ndash;Lens</strong>, where the away side&rsquo;s entire strip was produced under two coaches neither of whom picks the team tonight, and where the divisional home-win rate of 27.8% over 36 matches contradicts 46.1% over 306.</li>
<li><strong>What the confirmed team sheets changed, in one line per card.</strong> Bayern&ndash;Union: Neuer starts, closing the goalkeeping gap the card flagged, and Union&rsquo;s summer centre-back line is two out and one benched. Monza&ndash;Sassuolo: Monza name the eleven that was trailed; Aquilani resolves both selection questions the other way, with Bowie benched. Monaco&ndash;Lens: Monaco switch to a back three with Zakaria in it, Minamino and Salisu are fit substitutes, Ansu Fati is a new absence, and Cahuzac keeps the structure. Brentford&ndash;Chelsea: Ca&iuml;cedo and Jo&atilde;o Pedro out, Lavia benched, Chelsea in a 3-4-2-1, and Brentford&rsquo;s club-record signing starts on the bench. Espanyol&ndash;Elche: four of Espanyol&rsquo;s six summer loans are substitutes, Elche&rsquo;s injury list is one name, and the head coach on this card was wrong.</li>
</ul>"""

SOURCES = """Fixture list: the tab &ldquo;Slate 5&rdquo; inside slate.5.xlsx, read from the sheet XML (the workbook is strict-OOXML and opens as zero sheets in the normal reader); venue, kickoff and matchday confirmed against ESPN&rsquo;s public scoreboard for 18 September 2026. Club and league data: ESPN&rsquo;s public match record and season statistics objects for all five divisions and both seasons, plus ESPN&rsquo;s Serie B record for Monza&rsquo;s promotion campaign and a Serie B 2025&ndash;26 baseline computed over all 390 matches; Understat getLeagueData for xG, non-penalty xG, PPDA and deep completions. Referees: appointments from bundesliga.com and kicker (Brand), tuttomonza, forzamonza and sassuolonews (Perenzoni), lensois.com and horizonactu (Turpin), the Chelsea and Brentford club match pages (Madley), and iusport, lagrada and copeelche (Gonz&aacute;lez Esteban); records from playerstats.football profiles 507, 955, 208, 77 and 284, each of which independently listed tonight&rsquo;s fixture as that official&rsquo;s next appointment. Transfers and team news: fcbinside, sportschau and neunzigplus (Bayern, Union); madeinbrianza, lottomatica and sassuolonews (Monza, Sassuolo); maxifoot, dicodusport, lensois.com and befoot (Monaco, Lens); ESPN, the Yahoo and roundtable window reviews and chelseafc.com (Brentford, Chelsea); futbolfantasy (Espanyol, Elche); with previews from bundesliga.com, Sky Sport Italia, Sports Mole and comuniate. League baselines: baselines_0918.md, whose 2025&ndash;26 lines are carried from baselines_0917.md and baselines_0916.md. <strong>Confirmed team sheets, benches and injury lists for all five fixtures were supplied by you after the cards were written, and the Elche coaching change was then verified independently against TeleElx, Infobae, eldesmarque and the club&rsquo;s own announcement.</strong> Every source used is logged in full in sources_0918.md."""

out = shell
